{"asleep":{"id":"fecaa27e-5234-4d7a-a16a-ae9f04ed9eae","word":"asleep","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"in a state of sleep","word":"dormindo"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"inattentive","word":"dormindo"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"numb or prickling","word":"dormente"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"I was asleep when you called.","bold_text_offsets":[[6,12]],"type":"example","id":"679cb1bc-a014-453c-a490-e30260c04dcb"},{"text":"Never disturb a man asleep.","bold_text_offsets":[[20,26]],"type":"example","id":"d0a3f239-9e92-4080-b841-4453e5374301"}],"links":[["sleep","sleep"],["rest","rest"]],"glosses":["In a state of sleep; also, broadly, resting."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"tags":["not-comparable","predicative"],"id":"8fb069d7-ff64-4070-b8e3-776d8c2026ad"},{"examples":[{"text":"How could you miss that? Were you asleep?","bold_text_offsets":[[34,40]],"type":"example","id":"dabe6656-7e81-4883-835f-17dc6b386120"},{"text":"The reason they call it the American Dream is because you have to be asleep to believe it.","ref":"1997, George Carlin, Brain Droppings, New York: Hyperion Books, →ISBN, →LCCN, →OCLC, →OL, page 83:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[69,75]],"id":"bb545e56-5a99-4c7a-a5e9-6722dc60c12a"}],"categories":["English slang","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"links":[["Inattentive","inattentive"]],"raw_glosses":["(slang) Inattentive."],"glosses":["Inattentive."],"tags":["not-comparable","predicative","slang"],"id":"4b79585b-c00e-4f44-8396-020580d08ff2"},{"examples":[{"text":"My arm fell asleep. You know, like pins and needles.","bold_text_offsets":[[12,18]],"type":"example","id":"89802d51-3929-4454-a8c5-aa68aa1f414a"},{"text":"Louisa sat in the car crying, until her foot fell asleep. She shook her foot violently, afraid the numbness would turn to frostbite.","ref":"2003, No